# LogsTotal fleet configuration.
#
# Copy to deploy.env (gitignored), or let `./logstotal deploy:init -- cp.example.com w1.example.com`
# write it for you. Every deploy task reads it; an environment variable always wins over
# the value here.
#
# Parsed with grep and cut, never by a shell: no quotes, no inline comments, no
# indentation, no `export` prefix. Booleans must be spelled exactly `true` / `false`
# (or 1/yes/on) — `True` and `Yes` evaluate as false.
#
# None of these belong in .env: that file configures the application, this one
# configures the deployment.

# ── The fleet ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# First host = control plane, the rest = workers. An entry may be:
#   host          SSH as root
#   user@host     SSH as that user (needs write access to DEPLOY_REMOTE_DIR, or sudo -n)
#   local         THIS machine — commands run directly, no SSH. Lets the whole deploy
#                 run from the control plane itself.
DEPLOY_HOSTS=cp.example.com,w1.example.com,w2.example.com

# Install directory on every host.
# DEPLOY_REMOTE_DIR=/opt/logstotal

# SSH key. A leading ~ is expanded. Omit it to use the agent / ~/.ssh/config.
# SSH_IDENTITY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519

# ── Private network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEFAULT: wireconf. A multi-host fleet builds a WireGuard mesh unless you say otherwise.
#
# Without one, workers reach the control plane at its DEPLOY_HOSTS address — which means
# Redis, PostgreSQL and Garage are bound to a routable interface, protected only by their
# generated passwords. `./logstotal deploy:network` prints exactly which ports that opens.
#
# `wireconf` installs and drives https://github.com/wagga40/Wireconf to build a
# hub-and-spoke WireGuard mesh, control plane as hub. Tailscale needs an interactive
# login, so it stays manual — see docs/install/fleet.md.
#
# Two cases resolve to `none` on their own, because a mesh with no spokes protects
# nothing: a single-host fleet, and one where every host is this machine. An explicit
# DEPLOY_VPN=wireconf is always honoured.
# DEPLOY_VPN=wireconf
# A VPN that is asked for and cannot be built STOPS the deploy, rather than quietly
# falling back to the public addresses. Set this to accept that trade instead.
# DEPLOY_VPN_OPTIONAL=true
# The oldest Wireconf the deploy will run. Below 0.3.8 its remote commands run on a
# terminal, which makes it misread /etc/os-release and reject supported hosts. It is
# updated for you; set this only to run an older build deliberately.
# DEPLOY_VPN_MIN_VERSION=0.3.8
# WireGuard listen port. Written into wireconf.env as WG_PORT, named in the preflight's
# firewall check, and opened on the hub by deploy:bootstrap.
# DEPLOY_VPN_PORT=51820
# Add the hub's inbound ufw rule for that port. Defaults to true when DEPLOY_VPN=wireconf.
# Only the hub needs one — spokes dial out from ephemeral ports and ride conntrack.
# DEPLOY_OPEN_WG_PORT=true
# DEPLOY_VPN_NETWORK=10.200.0.0/24
# Install Wireconf when it is missing, and how long to wait for the tunnel to come up.
# DEPLOY_VPN_INSTALL=true
# DEPLOY_VPN_RETRIES=12
# DEPLOY_VPN_DELAY=5
# DEPLOY_VPN_HUB_ENDPOINT=cp.example.com

# The address workers use for the control plane. Discovered by default: the deploy asks
# a worker what the control plane's name resolves to, then asks the control plane for
# its own address. Set these only to override that.
# DEPLOY_CP_ADDRESS=10.200.0.1
# DEPLOY_CP_BIND_ADDRESS=10.200.0.1

# ── HTTPS and HTTP basic auth (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Setting DEPLOY_DOMAIN is what turns the Caddy proxy on for the control plane.
# DEPLOY_DOMAIN=logs.example.com
#
# How that proxy terminates TLS. Default acme.
#   acme      Let's Encrypt. Needs public DNS for DEPLOY_DOMAIN and inbound 80+443.
#   internal  Caddy's own CA — no DNS, no port 80, no internet. The air-gapped answer.
#             Install its root on client machines; leave ENABLE_HSTS off.
#   custom    Your certificate, from ./certs on the control plane.
#   off       Plain HTTP through Caddy, for a network where something in front already
#             terminates TLS.
# DEPLOY_PROXY_TLS=acme
# DEPLOY_ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com    # acme only
#
# DEPLOY_BASIC_AUTH_USER=admin
#
# The password is hashed on the CONTROL PLANE (it always has Docker) and only the
# bcrypt hash reaches a host's .env. Set it here, or pass it per run in the
# environment — the environment wins:
#   DEPLOY_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD='a long passphrase' ./logstotal deploy
# Set here, it is plaintext on this machine, so keep this file at mode 600. Setting
# the hash below instead means no plaintext is stored anywhere.
# DEPLOY_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD=
#
# Or supply the bcrypt hash yourself and skip the hashing step entirely. Generate one
# with:  docker run --rm -i caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password
# DEPLOY_BASIC_AUTH_HASH=$2a$14$...

# ── Which release to deploy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# By default a deploy ships THIS TREE, packaged. Two ways to ship a published release
# instead — the same two knobs `./logstotal upgrade` answers, because it is the same question:
#   VERSION=0.9.15 ./logstotal deploy        # resolve and download it, once, here
#   ARCHIVE=/media/usb/x.7z ./logstotal deploy   # exactly that file; no release lookup
# Neither belongs in this file: they describe one run, not the fleet.
#
# An explicit archive to push. Nothing is built.
# DEPLOY_PACKAGE=./logstotal-0.9.15.7z
# Force or forbid rebuilding the package. Unset means: reuse the archive here when its
# name matches this tree's VERSION and no source file is newer than it.
# DEPLOY_REBUILD=false

# ── Deploy behaviour ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Act on a subset only, leaving every other host running — how a worker joins a live
# fleet without restarting the control plane.
# DEPLOY_ONLY=w3.example.com

# DEPLOY_BOOTSTRAP=false          # hosts are already prepared; skip package installs
# The three the deploy sets to true for you. Listed because a hand-built sequence of the
# individual steps needs them, and the defaults are false when called that way.
# DEPLOY_STOP=true                # stop stacks before staging
# DEPLOY_START=true               # start them after
# DEPLOY_KEEPENV=true             # copy each host's .env off before staging, restore after
# A worker whose stack could not be CONFIRMED running fails the deploy. Off by default:
# the poll giving up is a statement about the wait, not about the worker.
# DEPLOY_STRICT=true
# Lines per host for ./logstotal deploy:logs.
# DEPLOY_LOGS_LINES=120
# Generate the per-host env files and stop, without installing any of them.
# DEPLOY_ENV_PUSH=false
# DEPLOY_ENV_PUSH_FORCE=yes       # overwrite an existing .env on a host
# DEPLOY_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# Where the generated per-host .env files and the fleet's secrets live (mode 0600).
# DEPLOY_ENV_DIR=deploy-envs
# Regenerate the shared secrets. ROTATES SECRET_KEY AND THE DATABASE PASSWORD, which logs
# every session out and leaves the workers authenticating with the old credentials.
# DEPLOY_ENV_FORCE=true
# DEPLOY_HUEY_WORKERS=4
# DEPLOY_KEEP_RELEASES=3
# DEPLOY_HEALTH_ATTEMPTS=30       # control-plane /health polling, x DEPLOY_HEALTH_DELAY
# DEPLOY_HEALTH_DELAY=2
# DEPLOY_SKIP_SMOKE=true

# Which preflight checks run. `pre` is the gate before bootstrap (reachability, remote
# shell, privilege, clock) with missing tools reported as INFO; `post` is the fatal tool
# check; `all` (default) is post's semantics, which is what `./logstotal deploy:preflight` runs.
# DEPLOY_PREFLIGHT_STAGE=all

# How `./logstotal deploy:plan` reports. `json` puts one document on stdout — settings, findings,
# per-host verdicts, counts — and the human check stream on stderr, so it can be piped.
# DEPLOY_PLAN_FORMAT=text

# Colour is on when the output is a terminal and off when it is a pipe, a file or CI.
# NO_COLOR=1 (the convention, not a DEPLOY_ setting) turns it off everywhere.

# ── Removal (./logstotal deploy:remove) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEPLOY_REMOVE_CONFIRM is ENVIRONMENT ONLY and is deliberately not read from this file:
# it is the confirmation, and a confirmation you wrote down once is not one. Type it on
# the command line, alongside the task's own prompt:
#   DEPLOY_REMOVE_CONFIRM=yes ./logstotal deploy:remove
#
# DEPLOY_REMOVE_KEEP_DATA=yes     # keep data/ uploads/ backups/, remove everything else
#
# DEPLOY_CLEAN is the lighter counterpart to a removal: `docker compose down -v --rmi all`
# on both stacks on every targeted host, leaving DEPLOY_REMOTE_DIR in place. It DELETES
# NAMED VOLUMES, databases included, so it needs DEPLOY_CLEAN_CONFIRM=yes on the command
# line — a confirmation, like the one above, and for the same reason.
# DEPLOY_CLEAN=true
# DEPLOY_REMOVE_VPN=yes           # also drop wg0, its config, and deploy-envs/vpn.json

# Print every command instead of running it. Connects to nothing.
# DEPLOY_DRY_RUN=true

# Deploy a tree older than the last release deployed from this machine. Refused by
# default: `./logstotal upgrade` stages nothing locally in package mode, so the checkout keeps
# naming the release it upgraded FROM — and a bare `./logstotal deploy` afterwards would push
# that over the fleet as an ordinary deploy.
# DEPLOY_ALLOW_DOWNGRADE=true

# ── Upgrades: where releases are published ───────────────────────────────────
# Which server `task upgrade:*` resolves releases from, and downloads them from.
#
# It is derived when it can be: a GitHub or Forgejo clone uses its own origin, and an
# archive built by CI carries the answer in `.release-origin`. Set it here when neither
# applies — most often when `origin` is an SSH URL, which has no web scheme or port, so
# `ssh://git@forge.example.com/you/LogsTotal.git` normalises to `https://forge.example.com/...`
# and misses the port the forge actually listens on.
# RELEASE_REPO_URL=http://forge.example.com:3000/you/LogsTotal

# The releases API, when it is not derivable from RELEASE_REPO_URL (GitHub and
# Gitea/Forgejo are detected automatically; anything else needs this).
# RELEASE_API_URL=https://forge.example.com/api/v1/repos/you/LogsTotal/releases
